endpoints.responseSchema:
TWO SHAPES, undeclared — the response carries no flag saying which one you got, so the client must detect it (see extractConfigGroups in src/hooks/asset-config/useAssetConfig.js).

(A) UNCONFIGURED asset — LIVE-CAPTURED 2026-08-03 from the browser (user pasted the real DevTools object). The groups ARE `features`, each carrying its own display copy:
  { success, message, data: { status: 'success', features: {
      template_selection: { name: 'template Selection', description: 'Select a template to configure the asset', configurations: [...] },
      observe:            { name: 'observe',            description: 'Observe the asset',                     configurations: [...] }
  } } }

(B) CONFIGURED asset — from the 2026-07-28 contract table (NOT independently live-verified). The groups move under `features.config` and `features` itself becomes saved-record metadata:
  { status, features: { config_id, template_id, asset_id, config: { template_selection: { configurations: [{ default }] } }, created_by, created_at, updated_at } }

Detection rule implemented: prefer `features.config` when it is an object, else treat `features` as the group map; in BOTH cases keep only entries whose `configurations` is an array (that array test, not a metadata-key denylist, is what filters out config_id/template_id/asset_id/created_by/created_at/updated_at — so a newly added metadata key cannot leak in as an empty section).
